body:
## Summary

In 0.4.0, `makeConfig()` (non-isolated mode) throws when the host environment contains two spellings of the same variable that only differ by case, even when the configuration never reads that variable:

```
Error: Ambiguous environment aliases for HTTP_PROXY
```

0.3.0 accepts the same environment.

## Reproduction

```js
// probe.mjs, with lino-arguments@0.4.0
import { makeConfig } from 'lino-arguments';
try {
  const c = makeConfig({
    yargs: ({ yargs, getenv }) =>
      yargs.option('verbose', { type: 'boolean', default: getenv('VERBOSE', false) }),
    argv: ['node', 'x'],
  });
  console.log('ok', c.verbose);
} catch (e) {
  console.log('THROW', e.message);
}
```

```
$ node probe.mjs
ok false
$ http_proxy=a HTTP_PROXY=a node probe.mjs
THROW Ambiguous environment aliases for HTTP_PROXY
```

The same happens with `npm_config_cache` + `NPM_CONFIG_CACHE` (set by npm/CI runners).

## Cause

`createConfigContext({ env: process.env, ... })` normalizes the whole of `process.env` via `normalizeEnvironment()` (`src/pure.js`), which throws on any case-insensitive duplicate key. Setting both `http_proxy` and `HTTP_PROXY` is common on Linux machines behind proxies and in CI images, so any CLI built on `makeConfig` fails at startup there.

## Expected

Only report ambiguity for keys the configuration actually reads (as `getenv()` does in 0.3.0), or fall back to a deterministic choice when the duplicates have equal values.

## Impact

link-assistant/agent stays on `lino-arguments@^0.3.0` until this is fixed (https://github.com/link-assistant/agent/pull/339).
